 ## [Unreleased]
 
+### Fixed
+- **`AnExplicitStopIsNotTreatedAsLostTime` could not tell a repaid pause from a missed
+  observation, and failed a documentation-only PR because of it (#154).** The sampler reads
+  positions out of the snapshot stream, and an entity is absent from a delta it did not
+  change while the frame itself still arrives on schedule — so two consecutive mentions of
+  the player can be two snapshot intervals apart with `lostFrames=0`, which is why the
+  existing lost-frame discard did not fire. Two intervals of legitimate travel read as one
+  step land on exactly `2.00x`, the value the threshold rejects: CI reported
+  `largest=0.6667 … top=0.6667@tick27(gap2)` against a server that had done nothing wrong.
+  Reproduced locally at `tick=32 gap=2 d=0.6667`.
+  - Each pair is now scaled to a single snapshot interval at judgement time
+    (`StepLog.MovingIntervals`), using the pair's own tick span — the gap was already being
+    collected and printed, but never acted on.
+  - The divisor is **clamped to the time since the resume**. Scaling by the raw gap was
+    rejected before for a good reason: a banked repayment puts a whole silent interval's
+    travel into one step and the previous mention is a whole silent interval earlier, so the
+    quotient comes back at one normal step and the defect measures as absent. The client was
+    provably not asking to move before the resume, so no elapsed time from before it may be
+    credited as travel, and a repaid pause keeps its full size.
+  - **The `2.0x` tolerance is unchanged.** Verified by disabling both explicit-stop paths in
+    `InputHandler` (the deadzone clear and the `MoveResult.None` backstop): the test still
+    fails at `4.0x` on both `60/15/5` and `15/15/5`, on a `gap8`/`gap48` pair scaled `over 1i`.
+  - A second mechanism, found while verifying the first under CPU load: **the test's own
+    send loop stalling**. It paced at a flat 66ms, which is one base tick at `15/15/5` and
+    one hold window at `60/15/5` — exactly where the server starts owing banked time — so
+    ordinary `Task.Delay` drift left a tick with no input and the elapsed-time step covered
+    two ticks in one, landing on the same 2.00x. Observed at `gap1 raw0.6667` (`15/15/5`)
+    and as a 9-base-tick step at `tick=121 gap4 raw0.7500` (`60/15/5`, with the process
+    starved and two frames lost in the same run) — both #100 behaving as specified.
+    - The client now sends **twice per world interval**, so only a real stall of a full
+      interval, not drift, can leave the server owed anything.
+    - The run measures its own worst send gap. A run whose send loop stalled past one world
+      interval is not evidence about the server, so the reading is discarded and retaken,
+      up to three times; if every attempt stalled the case **skips** (`SkippableTheory`,
+      the repo's real-skip rule) rather than reporting a verdict it cannot support. A
+      server that really repays a pause repays it on every attempt and its runs are not
+      stalled, so this costs the defect nothing.
+  - The diagnostic now prints the scaled value, the raw distance and the interval count per
+    sample, plus `maxSendGap` and the attempt number, so the next failure says which of the
+    three it was.
+
